From: Yehyeong Lee <yhlee@isslab.korea.ac.kr>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 15:01:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tftp: don't overflow the receive buffer after blocksize fallback

When a client requests a blksize smaller than 512 with -b, the receive
buffer is allocated at blksize + 4. If the server ignores the "blksize"
option (no OACK), the client falls back to a 512-byte blocksize and sets
io_bufsize = TFTP_BLKSIZE_DEFAULT + 4 without reallocating the buffer.

Buffer must be large enough for the fallback blocksize too.
---
 networking/tftp.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/networking/tftp.c b/networking/tftp.c
index 920f26937..3506cd640 100644
--- a/networking/tftp.c
+++ b/networking/tftp.c
@@ -320,7 +320,7 @@ static int tftp_protocol(
 	uint16_t recv_blk;
 	int local_fd = -1;
 	int retries, waittime_ms;
-	int io_bufsize = blksize + 4;
+	int io_bufsize = (blksize < TFTP_BLKSIZE_DEFAULT ? TFTP_BLKSIZE_DEFAULT : blksize) + 4;
 	char *cp;
 	/* Can't use RESERVE_CONFIG_BUFFER here since the allocation
 	 * size varies meaning BUFFERS_GO_ON_STACK would fail.
